@node Characters
@section Characters
Characters are objects that represent printed characters such as letters
and digits.  All Scheme implementations must support at least the
@value{ASCIIinitial} character repertoire: that is, Unicode characters
@nobr{U+0000} through @nobr{U+007F}.
Implemen@value{shy}ta@value{shy}tions may support any other Unicode
characters they see fit, and may also support non-Unicode characters as
well.  Except as otherwise specified, the result of applying any of the
following procedures to a non-Unicode character is
implementation-dependent.

Characters are written using the notation
@t{@nobr{#@backslashchar{}}@sv{character}} or
@t{@nobr{#@backslashchar{}}@sv{character name}} or
@t{@nobr{#@backslashchar{}}x@sv{hex scalar value}}.

The following character names must be supported by all implementations
with the given values.  Implementations may add other names provided
they cannot be interpreted as hex scalar values preceded by @code{x}.

@indentedblock
@ifnothtml
@multitable {xxbackspace} {; the preferred way to write a space}
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}alarm} @tab ; U+0007
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}backspace} @tab ; U+0008
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}delete} @tab ; U+007F
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}escape} @tab ; U+001B
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}newline} @tab ; the linefeed character, U+000A
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}null} @tab ; the null character, U+0000
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}return} @tab ; the return character, U+000D
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}space} @tab ; the preferred way to write a space
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}tab} @tab ; the tab character, U+0009
@end multitable
@end ifnothtml
@ifhtml
@multitable @columnfractions .2 .8
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}alarm} @tab ; U+0007
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}backspace} @tab ; U+0008
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}delete} @tab ; U+007F
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}escape} @tab ; U+001B
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}newline} @tab ; the linefeed character, U+000A
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}null} @tab ; the null character, U+0000
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}return} @tab ; the return character, U+000D
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}space} @tab ; the preferred way to write a space
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}tab} @tab ; the tab character, U+0009
@end multitable
@end ifhtml
@end indentedblock

Here are some additional examples:

@indentedblock
@ifnothtml
@multitable {xxx3bb} {xi (if character and name are supported)x}
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}a} @tab ; lower case letter
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}A} @tab ; upper case letter
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}@U{0028}} @tab ; left parenthesis
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{} } @tab ; the space character
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}x03BB} @tab ; @U{03BB} (if character is supported)
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}}@t{@charbody{iota}}
@tab ; @U{03B9} (if character and name are supported)
@end multitable
@end ifnothtml
@ifhtml
@multitable @columnfractions .15 .85
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}a} @tab ; lower case letter
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}A} @tab ; upper case letter
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}@U{0028}} @tab ; left parenthesis
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{} } @tab ; the space character
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}x03BB} @tab ; @U{03BB} (if character is supported)
@item @ht{#@backslashchar{}}@t{@charbody{iota}}
@tab ; @U{03B9} (if character and name are supported)
@end multitable
@end ifhtml
@end indentedblock

Case is significant in @nt{#\@sv{character}}, and in @nc{#\@sv{character
name}}, but not in @nt{#\x@sv{hex scalar value}}.  If @angle{character}
in @nt{#\@sv{character}} is alphabetic, then any character immediately
following @angle{character} cannot be one that can appear in an
identifier.  This rule resolves the ambiguous case where, for example,
the sequence of characters @samp{#\space} could be taken to be either a
representation of the space character or a representation of the
character @code{#\s} followed by a representation of the symbol
@code{pace}.

Characters written in the @samp{#\} notation are self-evaluating.  That
is, they do not have to be quoted in programs.

Some of the procedures that operate on characters ignore the difference
between upper case and lower case.  The procedures that ignore case have
@nobr{@samp{-ci}} (for @quotedbl{case insensitive}) embedded in their
names.

@deffn procedure char? obj

Returns @code{#t} if @var{obj} is a character, otherwise returns
@code{#f}.
@end deffn

@deffn procedure char=? @parI{char} @parII{char} @parIII{char} @value{defdots}
@deffnx procedure char<? @parI{char} @parII{char} @parIII{char} @value{defdots}
@deffnx procedure char>? @parI{char} @parII{char} @parIII{char} @value{defdots}
@deffnx procedure char<=? @parI{char} @parII{char} @parIII{char} @value{defdots}
@deffnx procedure char>=? @parI{char} @parII{char} @parIII{char} @value{defdots}

These procedures return @code{#t} if the results of passing their
arguments to @nc{char->integer} are respectively equal, monotonically
increasing, monotonically decreasing, monotonically non-decreasing, or
monotonically non-increasing.

These predicates are required to be transitive.
@end deffn

@deffn {char library procedure} char-ci=? @
@parI{char} @parII{char} @parIII{char} @value{defdots}
@deffnx {char library procedure} char-ci< @
@parI{char} @parII{char} @parIII{char} @value{defdots}
@deffnx {char library procedure} char-ci>? @
@parI{char} @parII{char} @parIII{char} @value{defdots}
@deffnx {char library procedure} char-ci<=? @
@parI{char} @parII{char} @parIII{char} @value{defdots}
@deffnx {char library procedure} char-ci>=? @
@parI{char} @parII{char} @parIII{char} @value{defdots}

These procedures are similar to @code{char=?} et cetera, but they treat
upper case and lower case letters as the same.  For example,
@code{(@nobr{char-ci=?} @nobr{#\A} @nobr{#\a})} returns @code{#t}.

Specifically, these procedures behave as if @nc{char-foldcase} were
applied to their arguments before they were compared.
@end deffn

@deffn {char library procedure} char-alphabetic? char
@deffnx {char library procedure} char-numeric? char
@deffnx {char library procedure} char-whitespace? char
@deffnx {char library procedure} char-upper-case? letter
@deffnx {char library procedure} char-lower-case? letter

These procedures return @code{#t} if their arguments are alphabetic,
numeric, whitespace, upper case, or lower case characters, respectively,
otherwise they return @code{#f}.

Specifically, they must return @code{#t} when applied to characters with
the Unicode properties Alphabetic, @nobr{Numeric_Type=Decimal},
@nobr{White_Space}, Uppercase, and Lowercase respectively, and @code{#f}
when applied to any other Unicode characters.  Note that many Unicode
characters are alphabetic but neither upper nor lower case.
@end deffn

@deffn {char library procedure} digit-value char

This procedure returns the numeric value (0 to 9) of its argument if it
is a numeric digit (that is, if @nc{char-numeric?} returns @code{#t}),
or @code{#f} on any other character.

@lisp
(digit-value #\3)     @ok{3}
(digit-value #\x0664) @ok{4}
(digit-value #\x0AE6) @ok{0}
(digit-value #\x0EA6) @ok{#f}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn procedure char->integer char
@deffnx procedure integer->char n

Given a Unicode character, @nc{char->integer} returns an exact integer
between 0 and @code{#xD7FF} or between @code{#xE000} and @code{#x10FFFF}
which is equal to the Unicode scalar value of that character.  Given a
non-Unicode character, it returns an exact integer greater than
@code{#x10FFFF}.  This is true independent of whether the implementation
uses the Unicode representation internally.

Given an exact integer that is the value returned by a character when
@nc{char->integer} is applied to it, @nc{integer->char} returns that
character.
@end deffn

@deffn {char library procedure} char-upcase char
@deffnx {char library procedure} char-downcase char
@deffnx {char library procedure} char-foldcase char

The @nc{char-upcase} procedure, given an argument that is the lowercase
part of a Unicode casing pair, returns the uppercase member of the pair,
provided that both characters are supported by the Scheme
implementation.  Note that language-sensitive casing pairs are not used.
If the argument is not the lowercase member of such a pair, it is
returned.

The @nc{char-downcase} procedure, given an argument that is the
uppercase part of a Unicode casing pair, returns the lowercase member of
the pair, provided that both characters are supported by the Scheme
implementation.  Note that language-sensitive casing pairs are not used.
If the argument is not the uppercase member of such a pair, it is
returned.

The @nc{char-foldcase} procedure applies the Unicode simple case-folding
algorithm to its argument and returns the result.  Note that
language-sensitive folding is not used.  If the character that results
from folding is not supported by the implementation, the argument is
returned.  See @value{UAXinitial}
#44@footnote{@value{refUnicodeAnnex44}} (part of the Unicode Standard)
for details.

Note that many Unicode lowercase characters do not have uppercase
equivalents.
@end deffn
